# keys(3)

## NAME

keys - key codes and key events for Lua

## SYNOPSIS

    keys.a ... keys.z
    keys.zero ... keys.nine
    keys.enter, keys.space, keys.backspace, keys.tab, keys.escape, keys.up, ...
    keys.getName(code)
    os.pullEvent("key")       -- "key", code, held
    os.pullEvent("key_up")    -- "key_up", code

## DESCRIPTION

lua(1) has a `keys` library (also `require("keys")`). It follows the `keys` API of CC: Tweaked: the codes are the same numbers (the key codes of GLFW), and `keys.getName(code)` returns the name of a code, or nil.

When a key of a keyboard that is connected to the computer is pressed, the program gets the event `key` (os(3)) with the code and `held` (true when the key repeats because it is held down). When the key is released, it gets `key_up` with the code. Compare the codes with `keys`:

    while true do
        local _, code = os.pullEvent("key")
        if code == keys.q then break end
        print(keys.getName(code))
    end

The names are those of CC: Tweaked, for example `leftCtrl`, `rightShift`, `numPad0`, `pageUp`, `grave`, `equals` and `f1`. `keys["return"]` is the same as `keys.enter`, and `keys.scollLock` (an old misspelling) the same as `keys.scrollLock`. Keys that the keyboard does not send (`f13` to `f25`) are also in the table.

## DIFFERENCES FROM CC: TWEAKED

- There are no `char` and `paste` events: the text that is typed is not given to Lua as events. To read text, use `io.read` (the terminal edits the line)
- `keys.escape` (256), `keys.world1` (161), `keys.world2` (162) and `keys.rightSuper` (347) are added. A key that is not in the table does not give an event
- A `key_up` may arrive without a `key` before it, and a `key` may have no `key_up` (for example after the world is loaded). After a `restored` event, treat all keys as released
- The events do not tell which keyboard was used. Events of other devices with the names `key-pressed` and `key-released` and the same values are also given as `key` and `key_up`
- The terminal also gets what is typed. While the main program waits with `os.pullEvent` or `os.pullEventRaw` with no filter, `key` or `key_up`, the terminal does not show the keys that make text (letters, Enter, Esc, the arrows and so on), and what was typed is thrown away. The terminal shows and keeps what is typed again when the program reads the standard input (`io.read`, the interactive prompt) or ends. This is done only when the standard input is the terminal and its mode is the default one. When the program is stopped (for example with Ctrl+C or kill(1)), the interactive shell restores the terminal. Text that arrives just after the program has started to read or has ended may still be shown and kept

